WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.236
-Our next guest is a "New York
Times" best-selling author

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:05.838
whose new novel "Our Missing
Hearts" is available now.

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:06.906
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:09.208
our friend Celeste Ng,
everybody!

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:10.943
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:17.683 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:24.423 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:26.092
Welcome back to the show!

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.094
-Thank you so much
for having me back on.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.430
-Last time we had you,
which is a ways back now,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.799
was for your book "Little Fires
Everywhere," which I loved.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:37.069
[ Cheers and applause ]
Which then became a television

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:39.371
miniseries with Reese
Witherspoon, Kerry Washington,

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.774
that you were an E.P. on.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:44.043
What was it like to -- I'm
assuming when you first wrote

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:46.279
the book you never had any
assumption it would be adapted.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:49.081
What was it like watching it
become a different medium?

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.484
-It was so surreal
in the best way.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:54.520
I mean, writing a novel, you're
by yourself in your office.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.821
It's really solitary.

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:59.058
And then a TV show has all these
people working on it.

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:00.259
It's such a team effort.

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.128
I mean, there's a room
full of writers.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.330
It was just a really cool
experience to see.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.767
-And obviously you now have
a relationship with Reese,

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.769
which is a pretty good person
to have a relationship with.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.139
Reese's book club have
already picked this book,

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:14.841
which is a pretty high praise.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.642
-I'm really honored. She does --

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:21.480
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.883
She does such great work,
I think, in trying to

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.418
lift up stories that get people

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:26.852
talking and having
conversations.

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:28.521
I really admire that about her.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:32.458
-Your new book takes place
in a near but dystopic future.

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:35.127
And it's sort of written
from the perspective

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.730
of a child named Bird.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.399
And I know you have
a son as well.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:43.203
When you write about a child,

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.238
is it hard to separate it
from your own?

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.606
Are you inspired by it?
Or is it very different?

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:50.376
-I learn a little bit from him
because having a kid,

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.445
I remember both
how smart kids are,

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:54.380
how perceptive they are.
They see stuff

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.282
and they don't have
all the filters that we have.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.950
So they just say it like it is.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.385
They can see
a little more clearly.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.054
And then you also remember how
kind of innocent they are,

00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:04.423
how there are some things that
just go right over their heads.

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:06.292
And it's both those things
at the same time.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.127
-And also the book is about
a relationship

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.796
with a mother and son.
When you write a book that's

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:13.166
about a mother and son, do you
ever worry that years from now

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.302
your son will read it and say,
"I never saw it like this"?

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.603
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:19.606
-A little bit, but you
know what? He might do that,

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.374
and then maybe he'll write his
own stories,

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:22.609
and that seems
like a good thing too.

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.477
-That's a pretty good thing.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.946
Is he too young to tell whether
or not he has any strength

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.382
as a storyteller or any
inclination towards it?

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.951
-He's always making up stories
and drawings,

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.686
but he claims that
he doesn't like reading.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.622
So I'm just kind of
not saying anything.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.923
I'm just going to see
how this comes out.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:39.492
-Do you feel like that's his way
of rebelling against

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.326
the fact that his mom
is an author?

00:02:41.326 --> 00:02:42.895
-I wonder that sometimes.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.329
He's like,
"I don't really like books."

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:45.531
And I'm like --

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:48.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.870
-It's a very funny position
to take.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.306
Like, "I'm not a book guy."

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.641
-That's the rebellion
you're taking?

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.642
-Yeah, that's the rebellion.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:59.612
I think -- Obviously, one of
the things that has inspired

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:03.549
this book and has happened
since we last saw each other,

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.284
the election of Donald Trump,

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:09.455
a lot of the rise in anti-Asian
hate crime here in this country.

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:12.725
Is writing about it, do you feel
like you're trying to inform,

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.794
trying to educate,
or is it catharsis?

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:16.162
How do you approach it?

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.530
-It's a little bit
all the above.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.632
I mean, I wasn't sure if I
wanted to put that into the book

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.334
because it's a dark thing
to think about.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.136
It's something I've been aware
of growing up

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:26.439
as a Chinese-American woman
that violence could be part of

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.140
my life at any point in time.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.609
But when we started seeing all
these things that are getting

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.678
caught on tape, people who are
just being beaten,

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:35.948
it felt really important to look
that right in the face

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:38.650
in the book, and say,
"Okay, if you didn't know

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.620
it was happening, now you know.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.955
And what are you going to do
about it?"

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:45.792
-With that said,
the book has a very sort of

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.426
optimistic tilt to it,
which I think

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:49.629
is important to let
readers know.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:52.565
Was that important as well as
you talk about, especially,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.402
you know, a slightly darker
future to make sure that

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:59.539
you also see the opportunity
for lightness at the end of it?

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:01.473
-Yeah, I'm so glad
you said that because a lot of

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:04.576
this book was me figuring out,
how do you hold on to hope

00:04:04.576 --> 00:04:06.979
when it feels like the world is
falling apart?

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:09.181
How do you give hope to your
children and encourage them

00:04:09.181 --> 00:04:11.150
to think that they can make
a difference

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:16.655
when it really feels like
everything is going badly?

00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:18.391
-Yeah.
-I just feel -- I'm like,

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:20.993
"Don't say that."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:25.398
-You wrote -- "Little Fires"
took place in Shaker,

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:26.732
where you lived.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:29.302
And now you live in Cambridge,
and it takes place there.

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:32.104
I think, especially when you're
talking about very real issues,

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:35.441
sometimes the communities you
write about might be a little

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:39.144
sensitive to the way
that you saw them.

00:04:39.144 --> 00:04:41.213
Do you feel like
that's happening

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:42.649
with this book as well
or do you worry

00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:44.650
that it happens with this book
or that is something you embrace

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:45.951
as part of
the writing challenge?

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:47.519
-Well, now I'm thinking
about it.

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:49.622
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:52.324
-I set the book in Cambridge
partly because it's my home.

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:54.727
It's been where I've lived
the longest of my adult life.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:57.029
But also because I think we
think about places like that

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:59.598
and in that the Northeast
as being a liberal bubble,

00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:02.034
and we have this idea, "Oh, that
stuff can't happen here."

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.504
But the truth is that
it happens everywhere.

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:08.006
And it felt like a way of sort
of asking people again to think,

00:05:08.006 --> 00:05:10.509
"Hey, it happens. What are
you going to do about it?"

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:14.280
-Now, I do feel, even though
you're one of our favorite

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.515
guests to be on the show,
the issue with authors is

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:18.684
I feel like we need to give you
five years to write another book

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.186
before you can come back.

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:22.020
-I keep thinking
I'm going to get faster,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.822
and I actually think
I'm getting slower.

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:25.858
[ Laughter ]
-Last time you were here,

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.859
"Little Fires"
barely had come out.

00:05:27.859 --> 00:05:30.529
And then it became --
let's just put it this way --

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:32.565
people on our staff were a lot
more excited you were here

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.500
this time than they were
the first time.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.969
Even when I came backstage to
your dressing room, a lot of

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.104
people were asking you
to sign this book for them.

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:41.507
And I think that speaks to
the success of that last book.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.909
When you sit down to write a new
one, does it make it a little

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:45.945
bit harder when the last one
is a book everybody read?

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.980
-A little bit. I mean,
the way I think about it is

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.015
more like I try and think of
people are in my corner

00:05:50.015 --> 00:05:52.251
and I want to do right by them.
I want to write a book that

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.385
they're going to be
excited about.

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:54.553
I don't want to
just phone it in.

00:05:54.553 --> 00:05:56.556
So if it takes longer,
that's why.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:58.190
-Well, I think we all
support that.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.858
And it's just so lovely to have
you here again.

00:05:59.858 --> 00:06:01.561
We will see you in --
If you need six,

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.463
we'll make it six, alright?
Thanks so much for being here.

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:06.599
You guys, Celeste Ng.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:08.734
"Our Missing Hearts"
is available now

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.469
wherever books are sold,
but please support

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:11.737
local and independent
bookstores.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:12.872
We will be right back.

